    examStyle: false,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["optimal truncation", "least term", "error function"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Write down the term at index \\( n \\).", workingLatex: "|t_n| = \\frac{(2n-1)!!}{(2x^2)^{n}}.", explanation: "Copy the erfc magnitude with the index symbolic. The numerator is the double factorial \\( (2n-1)!! = 1\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdots(2n-1) \\), the product of the first \\( n \\) odd numbers, and the denominator is a pure power of \\( 2x^2 \\). The combination \\( 2x^2 \\), not \\( x \\) alone, is the parameter that controls everything — worth fixing in mind from the start." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Write down the next term, at \\( n+1 \\).", workingLatex: "|t_{n+1}| = \\frac{(2n+1)!!}{(2x^2)^{n+1}}.", explanation: "Advance the index by one. In the numerator \\( (2n-1)!! \\to (2n+1)!! \\), which simply tacks the next odd number \\( 2n+1 \\) onto the product; in the denominator the exponent rises by one. Writing this out before cancelling keeps the double-factorial bookkeeping honest, which is where most errors with erfc occur." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Divide successor by predecessor.", workingLatex: "\\frac{|t_{n+1}|}{|t_n|} = \\frac{(2n+1)!!}{(2n-1)!!}\\cdot\\frac{(2x^2)^{n}}{(2x^2)^{n+1}}.", explanation: "Form the quotient and group as (double-factorial part) times (power part). The ratio is the right tool because, as for every series in this topic, the magnitudes fall to a single minimum then rise; we just need where that crossover sits." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Cancel the double factorial and the powers.", workingLatex: "\\frac{(2n+1)!!}{(2n-1)!!} = 2n+1, \\qquad \\frac{(2x^2)^{n}}{(2x^2)^{n+1}} = \\frac{1}{2x^2} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{|t_{n+1}|}{|t_n|} = \\frac{2n+1}{2x^2}.", explanation: "Stepping the double factorial up inserts exactly the one extra odd factor \\( 2n+1 \\), so the ratio of double factorials is just \\( 2n+1 \\); the powers give \\( (2x^2)^{-1} \\). The whole ratio collapses to \\( (2n+1)/(2x^2) \\) — again linear in \\( n \\), so a single crossing of 1." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Impose that the next term is smaller.", workingLatex: "\\frac{|t_{n+1}|}{|t_n|} < 1 \\iff \\frac{2n+1}{2x^2} < 1.", explanation: "The magnitudes shrink while this ratio stays under 1; the least term sits at the first index where it fails. We solve the inequality next." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Solve the inequality for \\( n \\).", workingLatex: "\\frac{2n+1}{2x^2} < 1 \\iff 2n+1 < 2x^2 \\iff n < x^2 - \\tfrac12.", explanation: "Multiply by \\( 2x^2 \\), subtract 1, then divide by 2. The minimum sits near \\( n \\approx x^2 \\): a key feature of erfc is that the least-term index tracks \\( x^2 \\), not \\( x \\). This quadratic scaling is why even a modest \\( x \\) gives a usefully deep minimum and high accuracy — far better than the \\( E_1 \\) series at the same \\( x \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 7, description: "Substitute \\( x = 2 \\) so \\( 2x^2 = 8 \\).", workingLatex: "n < x^2 - \\tfrac12 = 4 - \\tfrac12 = 3.5.", explanation: "With \\( x^2 = 4 \\) the bound is \\( n < 3.5 \\). Because \\( 3.5 \\) is not an integer there is no tie: the magnitudes strictly decrease for \\( n \\le 3 \\) and strictly increase from \\( n = 4 \\) onward. A non-integer bound is the clean case — no floor-versus-ceiling ambiguity to worry about." },
        { stepNumber: 8, description: "Confirm by bracketing the turning point.", workingLatex: "\\text{at } n=3:\\ \\frac{2(3)+1}{8}=\\frac{7}{8} < 1; \\qquad \\text{at } n=4:\\ \\frac{2(4)+1}{8}=\\frac{9}{8} > 1.", explanation: "Evaluate the ratio either side of the crossover, as an independent check on the inequality. At \\( n=3 \\) the ratio \\( 7/8 < 1 \\) means \\( |t_4| < |t_3| \\) (still improving up to \\( n=4 \\)); at \\( n=4 \\) the ratio \\( 9/8 > 1 \\) means \\( |t_5| > |t_4| \\) (worsening after). The descent therefore bottoms out exactly at \\( n=4 \\)." },
        { stepNumber: 9, description: "State the minimising index.", workingLatex: "n^\\ast = 4 \\qquad (\\approx x^2).", explanation: "The first index past the turning point — the smallest term — is \\( n^\\ast = 4 \\), squarely on the rule of thumb \\( n^\\ast \\approx x^2 = 4 \\). The non-integer bound \\( 3.5 \\) is reassuring: it removes the floor-vs-ceiling ambiguity that arises when \\( 2x^2 \\) is an exact integer and a tied pair appears (as it does, for instance, at \\( x=2.5 \\))." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( n^\\ast = 4 \\)",
      canonicalAnswer: "4",
    },
